  'published' => '<h1>About Symbulator</h1><div><h3>What is Symbulator?</h3><div>Symbulator (“<b>Symb</b>olic Circuit Sim<b>ulator</b>”) is a small program for the TI-89 calculator, widely regarded as the best simulator of linear circuit ever made for a calculator. It won 1st Place in the 2000 IEEE Student Paper Contest for Latin America.&nbsp;Symbulator&nbsp;can perform DC, AC, transient and frequency domain analyses of numerical and symbolic linear circuits; find the Thévenin, Norton and two-port equivalent of a network, and even do Bode plots. It accepts ideal resistors, inductors, mutual inductances, capacitors, independent and dependent current and voltage sources, op amps, transformers and all six types of two-ports.</div><div><br></div><h3>Why would I use it?</h3><div>A student of circuits theory, with Symbulator and the right knowledge, can solve most problems in circuit courses much faster and neater than ever before. Symbulator allows the student to focus in the conceptual understanding of circuit analysis, rather than the mathematical methods used for their solution. Symbulator offers a simpler way to define dependent sources, and includes special elements such as ideal transformers and six different two-ports, usually not included in other simulators. Its symbolic capabilities are outstanding.</div><div><br></div><h3>How much does it cost?</h3><div>Nothing. It’s free.</div></div>
',
